Migration wave planning divides a large migration project into manageable groups of workloads. Instead of moving an entire datacenter at once, organizations migrate workloads in controlled stages.

Migration waves can be organized according to:

* Application dependencies
* Business criticality
* Department
* Environment
* Technical complexity
* Risk level

For example, an organization may first migrate low-risk internal applications, followed by development environments, then customer-facing applications, and finally mission-critical systems.

A real-life use case is a multinational enterprise migrating hundreds of servers while maintaining business continuity throughout the project.

Wave-based migration makes complex cloud adoption easier to control, test, monitor, and troubleshoot.
